    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 33. Dust \u2013 Pretraining Transformers Without Backpropagation (Dahal, Mandal, G\u00fclbahar, Vegesna 2026, QLabs report)\n",
        "\n",
        "Source: qlabs.sh/research/dust (Oct 2026, no arXiv id assigned yet); code: github.com/qlabs-eng/dust (MIT). Claim: the first zeroth-order method competitive with backprop at pretraining transformer language models - Dust perturbs activations (node perturbation) independently at every token, so each token is a virtual population member and one forward pass evaluates the whole population in parallel.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What the paper does.** Add Gaussian noise to every linear layer's output independently per token; reward each token's noise by the loss drop it produced (centered across draws); the reward-weighted noise averaged over draws estimates the error at that layer's output (Eq. 2), and its outer product with the cached layer input is the weight gradient (Eq. 3) - the same outer product backprop forms at every Linear; only the error source differs (population reward vs chain rule). Attention internals (keys/values) are credited through the attention output over current and future tokens with decay gamma instead of the token's own loss. Interference control: separate passes per layer type, cached clean forward, the head jittered on cached logits. Reported results: tuned backprop matched/exceeded from a few hundred draws at 100k tokens and ~1k draws at 1M tokens; a power-law fit puts the 20M-token limit at 4.431 vs backprop's 4.633 (loosely constrained - the ladder is still falling); ~10^3-10^4x more compute-efficient than weight-space ES (EGGROLL) from 1M tokens (extrapolated); a 243M-parameter model outperforms a 120x smaller one at most population sizes; cosine(estimate, backprop) rises with population and stays aligned up to 1B tokens (fitted cos(K) = c/(1 + c/K)).\n",
        "\n",
        "**What runs here.** Dust is a training algorithm, so it lives where training lives: the s9 loop is an OPTAL pick - `Train::backprop` (default; byte-identical to the loop before this section existed) or `Train::dust` (this paper: the same schedule trained forward-only - the estimator sets `p.grad` from population rewards and the optimizer steps; no `.backward()` anywhere on that path, grep-able). It composes with every compiled selection: the estimator walks whatever modules the path defines, and with a gpt2bpe (50257) vocab the s9 Dust loop pretrains on a FineWeb slice (the paper's corpus, streamed at execution time; otherwise it keeps the section-7 corpus and records the exact why in the result JSON). This section then measures, on the live path and without mutating it: (1) the cosine ladder - mean cosine between the Dust estimate and the true backward gradient per layer type at populations 16/64/256 on one batch (the paper's Fig. 5 diagnostic; the reference comes from `torch.autograd.grad`, so neither weights nor `.grad` are touched and nothing is cloned); (2) held-out CE of the trained model - the paper's Table-1 test-loss metric - plus the s9 Dust training history when `Train::dust` produced this model. Simplifications vs the paper's tuned estimator - no 256-column head slabs, no per-block attention re-execution scoring (a decayed direct-loss credit instead), no residual-mixing scalar ES (this model has none), no multi-GPU population - are recorded in the code docstrings and in outputs/dust_results.json. Graded recreation (corpus entry 20, `graded/figures/qlabs_dust/`): every published table reproduced exactly - Fig.2/Table 1 ladder cells, Fig.4 overparameterization cells, Fig.5/App.G cosine-law fits, App.D/E/F tables - as 5 PNGs + metrics.json, with local Eq.1/2/3/4 probes (the Eq.2/3 probe fits Eq.5's law at RMSE 0.03)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT runner: Dust (QLabs 2026) diagnostics on the live path | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"Dust live: estimator cosine ladder (Fig. 5) + held-out CE of the trained model.\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Measurement only - no clones, no weight updates: training is the s9 Train::dust\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"impl (pick Train::dust to pretrain with Dust; Train::backprop stays the default);\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"the reference gradient here comes from torch.autograd.grad, so neither the live\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"weights nor .grad are touched. Paper-scale claims are cited, not rerun - exact\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"tuned estimator: qlabs-eng/dust dust.py.\"\"\"\n",
        "def run_dust_benchmark(model, ids, batch=4, ctx=None, gamma=0.98, chunk=8, seed=33,\n",
        "                       cosine_populations=(16, 64, 256),\n",
        "                       out_path=\"outputs/dust_results.json\", **_):\n",
        "    from pathlib import Path as _P33\n",
        "    dev = next(model.parameters()).device\n",
        "    ids = _t33.as_tensor(ids, dtype=_t33.long, device=dev)\n",
        "    ctx = int(ctx or min(int(getattr(model, \"ctx_len\", 64)), 64))\n",
        "    while ids.numel() < batch * (ctx + 1):\n",
        "        ids = _t33.cat([ids, ids])\n",
        "    nwin = ids.numel() // (ctx + 1)\n",
        "    wins = ids[: nwin * (ctx + 1)].view(nwin, ctx + 1)\n",
        "    n_eval = max(batch, nwin // 4)\n",
        "    train_wins, eval_wins = wins[: nwin - n_eval], wins[nwin - n_eval:]\n",
        "\n",
        "    def batch_at(i):\n",
        "        rows = _t33.stack([train_wins[(i * batch + b) % len(train_wins)] for b in range(batch)])\n",
        "        return rows[:, :-1].contiguous(), rows[:, 1:].contiguous()\n",
        "\n",
        "    def eval_batch(i):\n",
        "        rows = _t33.stack([eval_wins[(i * batch + b) % len(eval_wins)] for b in range(batch)])\n",
        "        xb, yb = rows[:, :-1].contiguous(), rows[:, 1:].contiguous()\n",
        "        with _t33.no_grad():\n",
        "            l = _dust_token_ce(_dust_logits(model, xb), yb)\n",
        "        mask = yb != -1\n",
        "        return float(l[mask].mean()) if bool(mask.any()) else None\n",
        "\n",
        "    t0 = _time33.time()\n",
        "    was_training = model.training\n",
        "    model.eval()\n",
        "\n",
        "    # 1) reference gradient via torch.autograd.grad (no .grad writes, no clone)\n",
        "    x0, y0 = batch_at(0)\n",
        "    true = {}\n",
        "    try:\n",
        "        uniq = list({id(p): p for p in model.parameters()}.values())\n",
        "        with _t33.enable_grad():\n",
        "            loss0 = _dust_token_ce(_dust_logits(model, x0), y0)\n",
        "        refs = _t33.autograd.grad(loss0[(y0 != -1)].mean(), uniq, allow_unused=True)\n",
        "        gmap = {id(p): g.detach().float() for p, g in zip(uniq, refs) if g is not None}\n",
        "        true = {n: gmap[id(p)] for n, p in model.named_parameters(remove_duplicate=False) if id(p) in gmap}\n",
        "    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - diagnostic only, never fatal\n",
        "        true = {}\n",
        "\n",
        "    # 2) cosine ladder: estimator vs the reference, per layer group (paper Fig. 5)\n",
        "    cosine = {\"populations\": [int(p) for p in cosine_populations],\n",
        "              \"groups\": {}, \"per_pop\": {}, \"skipped_sites\": []}\n",
        "    for pop in cosine_populations:\n",
        "        grads, meta = _dust_gradients(model, x0, y0, population=int(pop),\n",
        "                                      seed=seed + int(pop), gamma=gamma, chunk=chunk)\n",
        "        cosine[\"skipped_sites\"] = sorted(set(cosine[\"skipped_sites\"]) | set(meta[\"skipped_sites\"]))\n",
        "        per = {}\n",
        "        for name, g in grads.items():\n",
        "            t = true.get(name)\n",
        "            if t is None or g.shape != t.shape:\n",
        "                continue\n",
        "            c = float(_F33.cosine_similarity(g.reshape(1, -1), t.reshape(1, -1)).item())\n",
        "            per.setdefault(_dust_group_of(name), []).append(c)\n",
        "        cosine[\"per_pop\"][str(pop)] = {k: round(sum(v) / len(v), 4) for k, v in per.items()}\n",
        "        for k, v in per.items():\n",
        "            cosine[\"groups\"].setdefault(k, []).append(round(sum(v) / len(v), 4))\n",
        "    for k, v in cosine[\"groups\"].items():\n",
        "        if len(v) < len(cosine[\"populations\"]):\n",
        "            cosine[\"groups\"][k] = v + [None] * (len(cosine[\"populations\"]) - len(v))\n",
        "\n",
        "    # 3) held-out CE of the live model (the paper's Table-1 test-loss metric)\n",
        "    held = [eval_batch(i) for i in range(min(n_eval, 8))]\n",
        "    held = [h for h in held if h is not None]\n",
        "    heldout = {\"windows\": len(held), \"ce\": round(sum(held) / len(held), 4) if held else None,\n",
        "               \"metric\": \"mean CE on held-out windows of the measurement corpus (paper Table-1 test-loss metric)\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "    # 4) s9's Train::dust history, when that impl trained this model\n",
        "    hist = globals().get(\"dust_train_history\") or []\n",
        "    curve = {\"steps\": list(range(len(hist))), \"dust\": [round(float(x), 4) for x in hist],\n",
        "             \"backprop\": []}\n",
        "    if hist:\n",
        "        scope = (\"small scale only: s9 Train::dust (%d epochs x %d batches, population %d) + cosine ladder + held-out CE on the live model; paper-scale claims are cited, not rerun\"\n",
        "                 % (int(globals().get(\"n_epochs\", 0)), int(globals().get(\"batch_lim\", 0)),\n",
        "                    int(globals().get(\"dust_population\", 0))))\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        scope = \"small scale only: no Dust training in this selection (Train::backprop); cosine ladder + held-out CE on the live model; paper-scale claims are cited, not rerun\"\n",
        "\n",
        "    model.train(was_training)\n",
        "    data_src = globals().get(\"dust_data_source\",\n",
        "                             \"no Dust training in this selection (Train::backprop is the default)\")\n",
        "    measurements = {\n",
        "        \"loss_curve\": curve,\n",
        "        \"heldout\": heldout,\n",
        "        \"data\": {\"source\": data_src, \"tokens\": int(ids.numel()),\n",
        "                 \"vocab\": int(model.token_embedding.num_embeddings)},\n",
        "        \"cosine\": cosine,\n",
        "        \"estimator\": {\"populations\": [int(p) for p in cosine_populations],\n",
        "                      \"chunk\": chunk, \"gamma\": gamma,\n",
        "                      \"sigma\": {\"direct\": 0.2, \"kv\": 0.1, \"head\": 0.05},\n",
        "                      \"reference\": \"torch.autograd.grad on one batch (never .grad; no clone, no backward on the live path)\"},\n",
        "        \"paper_claims\": {\n",
        "            \"estimator\": \"paper: reward-weighted per-token noise averaged over draws estimates backprop's weight gradient\",\n",
        "            \"population\": \"paper: cosine(estimate, backprop) rises with population; Dust at 1k-16k draws matches tuned backprop at 1M-10M tokens\",\n",
        "            \"efficiency\": \"paper: ~10^3-10^4x more compute-efficient than weight-space ES (EGGROLL) from 1M tokens (extrapolated)\",\n",
        "            \"overparameterization\": \"paper: a 243M model outperforms a 120x smaller one at most population sizes\",\n",
        "        },\n",
        "        \"scope\": scope,\n",
        "    }\n",
        "    res = {\"paper\": \"qlabs.sh/research/dust (QLabs technical report 2026; code github.com/qlabs-eng/dust, MIT)\",\n",
        "           \"runner\": \"run_dust_benchmark\", \"seed\": seed,\n",
        "           \"measurements\": measurements, \"seconds\": round(_time33.time() - t0, 1)}\n",
        "    _P33(out_path).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n",
        "    _P33(out_path).write_text(_j33.dumps(res, indent=2, default=str), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n",
        "    return res"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "BENCH_RESULTS.append(live_call(\n",
        "    'dust', run_dust_benchmark, model=model, ids=bench_ids(globals(), 2048)))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Compatibility: Dust (QLabs 2026, qlabs.sh/research/dust; s9's selectable training algorithm - the file's first backprop-free path)\n",
        "\n",
        "**STACK:** the Train::dust impl of s9 runs over whatever path compiled (any Vocab/Scale/Context/Attn/PosEnc/Interface + KV @Impl active during its forwards); every eval/probe/KV section, because it trains the live model in place exactly as Train::backprop does - the model the suites see is the model s9 produced, not a copy; any ctx length (the s9 loop uses the compiled block_size; this section's window cap is ctx=64 by default, wider via the ctx= kwarg).\n",
        "**ALTERNATIVE (pick 1 per run):** training algorithm for the s9 loop - Train::dust (forward-only, this paper's algorithm) vs Train::backprop (default, byte-identical to the loop before this section existed) vs s16's own trainer; one Train value per run (OPTAL axis), arms never merged.\n",
        "**NEVER-SAME-PATH:** calling Train::dust a separate model (it is the compiled path, trained in place, forward-only); citing paper-scale protocol numbers (8x512 windows, 16k draws, EGGROLL comparisons) as measurements of this notebook - the FineWeb slice used here is the paper's corpus at notebook scale, never their 100M-parameter training; presenting the simplified estimator as the paper's tuned one (exact: qlabs-eng/dust dust.py).\n",
        "\n",
        "### Relations: Dust (QLabs 2026, s33) x All Previous Papers (standing rule)\n",
        "\n",
        "**vs LayerMix (arXiv:2608.28930, s10-12): STACK (untouched).** Probe heads read frozen states; Train::dust only changes the weights those probes read (in place, like backprop) - no other section's code path is touched.\n",
        "**vs SABER (arXiv:2608.27963, s13): STACK (untested).** Exit gating and forward-only training act at different times; a Train::dust pretrained model could feed the same exit loop.\n",
        "**vs Grokking (arXiv:2609.10657, s14/s16): STACK (closest training kin).** Both train weights during the run; s16 trains its own small models with full-batch recipes, s9's Train::dust trains the compiled model forward-only - same rollout discipline, disjoint code paths, never merged.\n",
        "**vs ConvMem (arXiv:2609.10441, s15): STACK (untested).** NIAH recall could benchmark a Train::dust pretrained model; the hierarchy math is inference-only.\n",
        "- **vs RiLM (arXiv:2609.10305, s19): STACK (readout only).** The Mobius readout applies to any head weights; Dust merely produces different ones.\n",
        "- **vs WRP (arXiv:2609.09883, s20): STACK (the other half of forward-free training).** WRP removes dimensions a forward pass says are absorbable; Dust trains weights a forward pass rewards - prune-after-dust and dust-after-prune both run, and the joint result (a pruned detector of its own gradient signal) is untested.\n",
        "- **vs TASCO (arXiv:2609.11393, s21): STACK in principle, different sites (name which moved).** TASCO is gradient-free prefix adaptation with weights frozen; Dust is backprop-free weight adaptation with the prefix frozen - the file's two \"no-backprop\" families. A head-to-head accuracy claim must name the site (prefix vs weights).\n",
        "**vs Ignorance (arXiv:2609.02959, s22): STACK (readout only).** Temperature priors on a Train::dust pretrained model are a valid measurement; no shared code path.\n",
        "- **vs SharedCircuits (arXiv:2609.04463, s23): STACK (diagnostic).** AP gradients select circuits via backprop on the live model; Dust's estimate is a backprop-free attribution candidate for the same selection - do population rewards find the same circuit? A genuinely new comparison.\n",
        "- **vs CitationGraph (s24): NONE.** Meta-section.\n",
        "- **vs ImplicitPatch (arXiv:2511.17864, s25): STACK (different update algebras).** ImplicitPatch compiles context into rank-1 weight updates; Dust estimates weight updates from rewards. Both change weights outside backprop; neither replaces the other's site.\n",
        "- **vs LBI (arXiv:2605.09204, s26): the file's direct contrast pair (never same-site).** LBI parallelizes backprop's backward scan; Dust deletes the backward entirely - same objective (cheap gradients to all layers), opposite mechanisms. The cosine-to-backprop ladder of this section is the bridge metric LBI never needed.\n",
        "- **vs KVCache survey + SpindleKV/KeepKV/CQ/EpiCache (arXiv:2603.20397, s27-31): STACK with a shared knob.** Dust reads rewards from whatever forward ran - under a KV policy the reward signal is exactly the policy's belief, so the cosine ladder under each @Impl is a cheap selector for which compressions stay trainable. Pick one policy per run as usual; nothing conflicts.\n",
        "**vs Loop decode (arXiv:2605.23872, s32): STACK (the paper's own future-work hook).** Dust defers to \"transformers looped over many steps that backpropagation through time struggles to train\"; the notebook has s32's LoopWrapper and s26's gradient-region partition - Train::dust over a looped forward is the experiment both papers ask for and neither ran.\n",
        "- **vs Attention (arXiv:1706.03762) + BERT (arXiv:1810.04805, graded-only): the crown relation, detailed in the foundation note below.** Dust consumes the first paper's forward and never builds its backward."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Backprop: Dust (QLabs 2026, s33) x the first paper (arXiv:1706.03762, foundation; no prior line touched)\n",
        "\n",
        "- **The strongest possible relation, and it is adversarial: Dust is the first section that runs the first paper's forward and refuses to build its backward.** Everything the estimator touches is a first-paper primitive - `attn_scores`/`attn_combine` under the causal tril mask, the `QKVLinear` projections, the log-softmax head (per-token CE is a gather over it), and the position table. Backprop's chain rule through that exact graph is what the paper deletes; the outer-product form `error x input` (the same product backprop forms at every Linear) survives, fed by population rewards instead of derivatives. From the first paper's perspective: every prior section consumed its forward - this one consumes it twice as hard and throws away the backward.\n",
        "- **What can be changed considering both (levers; additive, none claimed as run):** (1) this section's cosine ladder is the paper's Fig. 5 run on the first paper's compiled blocks; extending the ladder to every Attn/PosEnc @Impl turns it into a per-path \"backprop-freeness\" readout the paper only has for one architecture. (2) the KV sections (s27-31) define alternative forwards whose rewards are read from compressed beliefs; the same ladder under each policy selects compressions that stay trainable - a joint knob neither paper had alone. (3) s32's LoopWrapper and s26's LBI are the two backprop-pressure surfaces (BPTT, backward scans); Dust-over-loop removes BPTT from that experiment entirely.\n",
        "**Future directions to make them more compatible:** a `dust_step` variant reusing cached logits to skip the extra clean forward (the eval path already caches); the paper's exact attention-internal scoring (re-run the block, score delta-o against the estimated output error, Eq. 4) to lift the attention-group cosine; 256-column slab jitter for the head at gpt2bpe scale (the paper's exact head treatment for large vocab); drive s28 suite accuracy from the Train::dust gpt2_124m chain to price \"population sufficiency\" in benchmark points instead of loss.\n",
        "**Boundary kept:** the Train::dust path never calls `.backward()` (the estimator cell is AST-clean); the measurement path's reference gradient uses `torch.autograd.grad` (never `.grad`, never a clone) - it is the paper's own chosen reference (Fig. 5), and s33 mutates nothing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Ref-graph line (independent citation check): zero shared references - 1706.03762 is absent from Dust's ~30-entry reference list (Rumelhart 1986, LeCun 1988, Salimans 2017, Nesterov 2017, Malladi 2023, Sarkar 2025 EGGROLL, Sutton 2019's bitter lesson, the authors' own Vegesna & Dahal 2025), i.e. Dust cites the backprop/zeroth-order lineage, not the attention lineage. The missing edge is the substrate itself: a training algorithm for the architecture the first paper defines, scheduled for the compute-rich regime. Rewritten for the foundation lens: backprop-free search as the first paper's training story after the chain rule.\n",
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 10. Hallucination Probing \u2013 The Hallucination Signal Is a Mean Shift: Why Simple Probes Suffice (Lee, Seo, Lim 2026, arXiv:2608.28930; LayerMix method)\n",
        "",
        "**Paper in one paragraph.** Hidden-state probes detect LLM hallucinations, but recent work piles on architectural complexity (cross-layer tracking, optimal-transport pseudo-labels, attention-gated subspaces) without characterising the signal geometry. This paper measures it in the controlled **paired-example paradigm** (factual vs hallucinated input pairs, 3x 7B models x 3 datasets: TruthfulQA N=4,135, HaluEval-Dialogue N=20,000, FaithDial N=5,848) and finds the signal is overwhelmingly **one mean-shift direction** \\(\\\\delta = \\\\mu_1 - \\\\mu_0\\\\): deleting it collapses detection to chance (0.499 AUROC); projecting onto it alone recovers 0.834; shrinkage LDA closes ~73% of the remaining Fisher gap, so apparent complexity is high-dimensional covariance-estimation noise, not non-linearity. A plain **L2-regularized logistic regression (C=0.001, 0.952 AUROC)** bounds or beats 12 engineered alternatives, and the signal spans a **contiguous layer band** (best layer varies: L14 Llama, L16 Mistral, L18 Qwen), so **LayerMix** \\u2014 CV-score every layer, keep top-K=5, train one probe per layer, average predictions \\u2014 matches oracle-layer performance (0.954) with ~35s overhead. Scope warning from the authors: paired-example only; the direction does NOT transfer to free-form generation (their pilot: 0.477) and mean shifts are near-orthogonal across datasets (cos~0.12).\n",
        "",
        "**How it helps this repo.** nanoGPT (char-level tiny-Shakespeare) cannot reproduce 7B QA numbers, but it adopts the *method*: freeze model \\u2192 last-token hidden states \\u2192 StandardScaler \\u2192 stratified 5-fold CV \\u2192 L2-LR probe. That simple probe is the bar every future detector must beat. LayerMix ports directly to the 3-layer nanoGPT stack (CV top-K + averaging, no oracle access). Diagnostics port too: length-confound check, mean-shift ablation, layer-wise AUROC curves, label-efficiency curves.\n",
        "",
        "**How it interacts with OPTAL.** Probes sit ON TOP of frozen hidden states extracted via forward hooks \\u2014 zero changes to nanoGPT logic, compatible with EVERY existing impl combo (matmul/addernet, layernorm/regularisedbn, decoder/encdec, vanilla/mqa/gqa/mla/dsa/tokenformer/tpa). Bundle rule: probe heads, corruption ops, extractor, and diagnostics are plain ALWAYS-PRESENT codeblocks (no Impl tags) that must exist in EVERY compiled path; branching them would retrain one model per probe for no reason. They share one site: N\u00d7d states + labels \\u2192 score). Branching is reserved for model-defining axes: Model/State/Arch/Attn/Vocab/Scale/Context. Exotic paper baselines (DoLa, HaloScope, CLAP, ...) stay as `@Graded` published-number checks in `graded/papers/03_*.optll`.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "\"\"\"Paired-example dataset pipeline (LayerMix paper \\u00a75, common code, no Impl).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Paper datasets: TruthfulQA (TQA, Lin et al. 2022b, N=4,135 QA pairs), \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"HaluEval-Dialogue (HE, Li et al. 2023, N=20,000 dialogue turns), \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"FaithDial (FD, Dziri et al. 2022, N=5,848 info-seeking dialogues). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Paradigm: PAIRED examples \\u2014 each prompt appears once factual (y=0) and once \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"hallucinated (y=1). This isolates representational geometry from \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"generation-induced distribution shift (paper \\u00a75 Protocol). Hidden states are \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"extracted at the LAST token position of each input sequence. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"nanoGPT adaptation: this repo is char-level tiny-Shakespeare, so the load is \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"offline-safe by default (synthetic Gaussian paired corpus with a planted mean \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"shift, same geometry the paper measures, tokenized through the repo char \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"vocabulary); pass use_hf=True explicitly to try HuggingFace `datasets` first \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"(offline run still falls back to synthetic). No model logic is touched.\"\"\"\n",
        "def build_paired_corpus(dataset=\"TQA\", n_pairs=500, seed=0, use_hf=False):\n",
        "    import random\n",
        "    rng = random.Random(seed)\n",
        "    if use_hf:\n",
        "        try:\n",
        "            from datasets import load_dataset\n",
        "            name_map = {\n",
        "                \"TQA\": (\"truthfulqa/truthful_qa\", \"generation\"),\n",
        "                \"HE\": (\"pminervini/HaluEval\", \"dialogue\"),\n",
        "                \"FD\": (\"McGill-NLP/FaithDial\", None),\n",
        "            }\n",
        "            repo, conf = name_map.get(dataset, name_map[\"TQA\"])\n",
        "            ds = load_dataset(repo, conf, split=\"validation\" if dataset == \"TQA\" else \"test\")\n",
        "            pairs = []\n",
        "            for row in ds:\n",
        "                q = row.get(\"question\", row.get(\"prompt\", row.get(\"history\", \"\")))\n",
        "                good = row.get(\"best_answer\", row.get(\"correct_answer\", row.get(\"response\", \"\")))\n",
        "                bad = row.get(\"incorrect_answers\", row.get(\"hallucinated_response\", [\"?\"]))\n",
        "                bad = bad[0] if isinstance(bad, list) else bad\n",
        "                if q and good and bad:\n",
        "                    pairs.append((str(q), str(good), str(bad)))\n",
        "                if len(pairs) >= n_pairs:\n",
        "                    break\n",
        "            if pairs:\n",
        "                return pairs\n",
        "        except Exception as e:\n",
        "            print(f\"  HF load failed ({e}); using synthetic paired corpus.\")\n",
        "    topics = [\"Romeo\", \"dreams\", \"love\", \"night\", \"sword\", \"crown\"]\n",
        "    pairs = []\n",
        "    for i in range(n_pairs):\n",
        "        t = topics[i % len(topics)]\n",
        "        q = f\"Tell of {t}: \"\n",
        "        good = f\"{t} is true in the play. \"\n",
        "        bad = f\"{t} is false in the play. \"\n",
        "        pairs.append((q, good, bad))\n",
        "    return pairs\n",
        "\n",
        "def encode_paired(pairs, ctx_len):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Encode each (question, good, bad) into two fixed-length int rows.\"\"\"\n",
        "    rows, labels = [], []\n",
        "    for q, good, bad in pairs:\n",
        "        for ans, y in ((good, 0), (bad, 1)):\n",
        "            s = (q + ans)[-ctx_len:]\n",
        "            ids = [token_to_int.get(c, 0) for c in s]\n",
        "            ids = [0] * (ctx_len - len(ids)) + ids\n",
        "            rows.append(ids)\n",
        "            labels.append(y)\n",
        "    import torch as _t\n",
        "    return _t.tensor(rows, dtype=_t.long), _t.tensor(labels, dtype=_t.long)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "\"\"\"Frozen hidden-state extractor + CV/AUROC utils (common code, no Impl).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Paper \\u00a75: hidden states at the last token; StandardScaler fit on train only; \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"stratified 5-fold CV; LayerMix scoring uses nested CV inside training folds. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Implementation uses forward hooks on `model.blocks` (and `model.encoder` when \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"present) so NO model logic changes. Works for NanoGPT and \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"EncoderDecoderTransformer under every OPTAL impl combo.\"\"\"\n",
        "def extract_last_token_states(model, xb, layer_ids=None):\n",
        "    model.eval()\n",
        "    feats = {}\n",
        "    hooks = []\n",
        "    from nanogpt_pipeline import decoder_stack as _dstk, compat_model as _cm\n",
        "    _cm(model)\n",
        "    blocks = list(_dstk(model))\n",
        "    if hasattr(model, \"encoder\"):\n",
        "        blocks = list(model.encoder) + [b for b in blocks if b not in list(model.encoder)]\n",
        "    keep = set(layer_ids) if layer_ids is not None else None\n",
        "    for li, blk in enumerate(blocks):\n",
        "        if keep is not None and li not in keep:\n",
        "            continue\n",
        "        hooks.append(blk.register_forward_hook(\n",
        "            lambda m, inp, out, _li=li: feats.setdefault(_li, out.detach())))\n",
        "    with torch.no_grad():\n",
        "        _ = model(xb.to(next(model.parameters()).device))\n",
        "    for h in hooks:\n",
        "        h.remove()\n",
        "    return {li: feats[li][:, -1, :].float().cpu() for li in sorted(feats)}\n",
        "\n",
        "def standardize_fit_apply(Xtr, Xte):\n",
        "    mu, sd = Xtr.mean(0, keepdim=True), Xtr.std(0, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-12)\n",
        "    return (Xtr - mu) / sd, (Xte - mu) / sd\n",
        "\n",
        "def stratified_folds(y, k=5, seed=0):\n",
        "    g = torch.Generator().manual_seed(seed)\n",
        "    folds = [[] for _ in range(k)]\n",
        "    for c in y.unique():\n",
        "        idx = (y == c).nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]\n",
        "        idx = idx[torch.randperm(len(idx), generator=g)]\n",
        "        for i, j in enumerate(idx.tolist()):\n",
        "            folds[i % k].append(j)\n",
        "    return [torch.tensor(f, dtype=torch.long) for f in folds]\n",
        "\n",
        "def auroc_score(y_true, y_score):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Mann-Whitney AUROC (no sklearn dependency).\"\"\"\n",
        "    y_true = y_true.reshape(-1).double()\n",
        "    y_score = y_score.reshape(-1).double()\n",
        "    order = torch.argsort(y_score)\n",
        "    ranks = torch.empty_like(order, dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "    ranks[order] = torch.arange(1, len(order) + 1, dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "    n1, n0 = y_true.sum(), (1 - y_true).sum()\n",
        "    if n1 == 0 or n0 == 0:\n",
        "        return 0.5\n",
        "    return float((ranks[y_true == 1].sum() - n1 * (n1 + 1) / 2) / (n1 * n0))\n",
        "\n",
        "def cv_auroc_for_probe(X, y, fit_predict, k=5, seed=0):\n",
        "    aucs = []\n",
        "    for fold in stratified_folds(y, k, seed):\n",
        "        te = fold\n",
        "        tr = torch.tensor([i for i in range(len(y)) if i not in set(te.tolist())])\n",
        "        Xtr, Xte = standardize_fit_apply(X[tr], X[te])\n",
        "        aucs.append(auroc_score(y[te], fit_predict(Xtr, y[tr], Xte)))\n",
        "    return float(sum(aucs) / len(aucs)), aucs\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "\"\"\"Mean-shift geometry + linear probes (Paper \\u00a73, Table 1/2/8).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 delta = mu1 - mu0 (class centroids on train fold ONLY, per paper \\u00a73.1, to avoid \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  in-sample artifacts). Removing delta collapses detection to chance (0.499). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 1D projection onto delta: 0.834 AUROC (78% of above-chance unreg-LR signal). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 Fisher LDA gap (Table 2): raw 1D 0.834 vs L2-LR 0.952 (gap +0.118); shrinkage \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  LDA (Ledoit-Wolf 2004) reaches 0.920, closing ~73% \\u2014 the rest is covariance- \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  estimation noise, not non-linearity. ZCA whitening HURTS (0.790). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 Operational baseline: L2-regularized LR with C=0.001 (Fig.5 optimum). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  All heads share one site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores. Each head below \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  lives in its own single-Impl cell.\"\"\"\n",
        "def class_centroids(X, y):\n",
        "    return X[y == 0].mean(0), X[y == 1].mean(0)\n",
        "\n",
        "def mean_shift_delta(X, y):\n",
        "    mu0, mu1 = class_centroids(X, y)\n",
        "    return mu1 - mu0\n",
        "\n",
        "def remove_mean_shift(X, y):\n",
        "    d = mean_shift_delta(X, y)\n",
        "    d = d / d.norm().clamp_min(1e-12)\n",
        "    return X - (X @ d).unsqueeze(1) * d.unsqueeze(0)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: l2lr_fit_predict | Paper s3, Tables 1-2-8, Fig.5 | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"L2-regularized logistic regression probe (Paper operational baseline, C=0.001).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Mean 0.952 AUROC; bounds all 12 alternatives. L2 shrinkage suppresses noise in \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"the residual dims (Fig.5 optimum C=0.001).\"\"\"\n",
        "def l2lr_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte, C=0.001, steps=2000, lr=0.5):\n",
        "    w = torch.zeros(Xtr.shape[1], dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "    b = torch.zeros((), dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "    lam = 1.0 / (C * len(Xtr))\n",
        "    Xd, yd = Xtr.double(), ytr.double()\n",
        "    for _ in range(steps):\n",
        "        p = torch.sigmoid(Xd @ w + b)\n",
        "        g = p - yd\n",
        "        w -= lr * (Xd.t() @ g / len(Xd) + lam * w)\n",
        "        b -= lr * g.mean()\n",
        "    return torch.sigmoid(Xte.double() @ w + b).float()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: fisherlda_fit_predict | Paper Table 2 Fisher column, App C | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"Fisher LDA probe (Paper Table 2 Fisher column, App C).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Projects onto w_F = Sw^-1 delta. Mean 0.834 1D; gap +0.118 to L2-LR.\"\"\"\n",
        "def fisherlda_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte, reg=1e-6):\n",
        "    Xd = Xtr.double()\n",
        "    mu0, mu1 = Xd[ytr == 0].mean(0), Xd[ytr == 1].mean(0)\n",
        "    Xc = torch.cat([Xd[ytr == 0] - mu0, Xd[ytr == 1] - mu1])\n",
        "    Sw = (Xc.t() @ Xc) / len(Xc) + reg * torch.eye(Xd.shape[1], dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "    w = torch.linalg.solve(Sw, (mu1 - mu0))\n",
        "    return (Xte.double() @ w).float()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: shrinkagelda_fit_predict | Paper s3.2, closes 73 percent of Fisher gap, 0.920 AUROC | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"Shrinkage LDA probe (Ledoit-Wolf style, Paper closes 73 percent of Fisher gap).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Reaches 0.920 mean AUROC; residual 0.032 may be non-Gaussian structure.\"\"\"\n",
        "def shrinkagelda_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte, alpha=0.5):\n",
        "    Xd = Xtr.double()\n",
        "    mu0, mu1 = Xd[ytr == 0].mean(0), Xd[ytr == 1].mean(0)\n",
        "    Xc = torch.cat([Xd[ytr == 0] - mu0, Xd[ytr == 1] - mu1])\n",
        "    Sw = (Xc.t() @ Xc) / len(Xc)\n",
        "    tr = Sw.diag().mean()\n",
        "    Sw_s = (1 - alpha) * Sw + alpha * tr * torch.eye(Xd.shape[1], dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "    w = torch.linalg.solve(Sw_s, (mu1 - mu0))\n",
        "    return (Xte.double() @ w).float()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "\"\"\"Subspace probes (Paper \\u00a76 Table 16, App L; Table 3 oracle-layer rows).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Taxonomy the paper verifies: mean-targeting beats variance-targeting. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 PLS-DA k=2: 0.910 (mean-targeting, but truncated; full-dim wins by 0.044). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  PLS-DA comp.1 aligns with delta by construction in binary case (Barker-Rayens). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 SVD+LR k=100: 0.929 (variance-targeting, loses mean shift). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 MeanDiff (Zou et al. 2023 representation-engineering direction): 0.893. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"\\u2022 gcPCA/KM-Unsup: 0.699/0.765 (variance-targeting, worst). Full-dim L2-LR 0.952.\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  Each head below lives in its own single-Impl cell.\"\"\"\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: meandiff_fit_predict | Paper Table 16 (0.893), mean-targeting | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"MeanDiff probe (representation-engineering direction, Paper Table 16: 0.893).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Score = X dot delta-hat. Mean-targeting; beats all variance methods.\"\"\"\n",
        "def meandiff_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte):\n",
        "    d = mean_shift_delta(Xtr.double(), ytr)\n",
        "    w = (d / d.norm().clamp_min(1e-12)).float()\n",
        "    return (Xte.float() @ w)\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: svd_fit_predict, k=100 | Paper Table 16 (0.929), variance-targeting | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"SVD+LR probe k=100 (Paper Table 16: 0.929, variance-targeting).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Top-k PCA then L2-LR. Loses mean shift; loses to full-dim by 0.023.\"\"\"\n",
        "def svd_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte, k=100):\n",
        "    mu = Xtr.double().mean(0, keepdim=True)\n",
        "    U, S, Vh = torch.linalg.svd(Xtr.double() - mu, full_matrices=False)\n",
        "    Vk = Vh[:min(k, Vh.shape[0])].t()\n",
        "    return l2lr_fit_predict((Xtr.double() @ Vk).float(), ytr, (Xte.double() @ Vk).float())\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: plsda_fit_predict, k=2 | Paper Table 16 (0.910), mean-targeting truncated | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"PLS-DA probe k=2 (Paper Table 16: 0.910, mean-targeting but truncated).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Component 1 aligns with delta by construction (Barker-Rayens 2003).\"\"\"\n",
        "def plsda_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte, k=2):\n",
        "    Xd = Xtr.double()\n",
        "    yc = (ytr.double() - ytr.double().mean()).unsqueeze(1)\n",
        "    Xc = Xd - Xd.mean(0, keepdim=True)\n",
        "    U, S, Vh = torch.linalg.svd(Xc.t() @ yc, full_matrices=False)\n",
        "    W = Vh[:1].t()\n",
        "    for _ in range(1, min(k, Xd.shape[1])):\n",
        "        T = Xc @ W\n",
        "        P = (Xc.t() @ T) / (T.t() @ T).clamp_min(1e-12)\n",
        "        Xc = Xc - T @ P.t()\n",
        "        U2, S2, Vh2 = torch.linalg.svd(Xc.t() @ yc, full_matrices=False)\n",
        "        W = torch.cat([W, Vh2[:1].t()], dim=1)\n",
        "    return l2lr_fit_predict((Xtr.double() @ W).float(), ytr, (Xte.double() @ W).float())\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT probe head: mlp_fit_predict | Paper s6.3, App I, Table 14, boundary linear | site: (N,d) states + labels -> scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"MLP probe = SAPLMA-style baseline (Paper \\u00a76.3, App I, Table 14).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Controlled MLP (256 units, ReLU, dropout 0.3) on the oracle layer differs from \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"L2-LR by |delta|<=0.002 across all 9 conditions (mean both 0.952): the boundary \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"is overwhelmingly LINEAR in the paired-example paradigm, so SAPLMA's 2-layer \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"MLP (0.919, under-regularized) loses to properly regularized L2-LR (0.952).\"\"\"\n",
        "class _SaplmaMlp(torch.nn.Module):\n",
        "    def __init__(self, d, h=256, p=0.3):\n",
        "        super().__init__()\n",
        "        self.net = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(d, h), torch.nn.ReLU(), torch.nn.Dropout(p), torch.nn.Linear(h, 1))\n",
        "    def forward(self, x):\n",
        "        return self.net(x).squeeze(-1)\n",
        "\n",
        "def mlp_fit_predict(Xtr, ytr, Xte, h=256, epochs=50, lr=1e-3):\n",
        "    m = _SaplmaMlp(Xtr.shape[1], h).float()\n",
        "    opt = torch.optim.Adam(m.parameters(), lr=lr)\n",
        "    loss_fn = torch.nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()\n",
        "    m.train()\n",
        "    for _ in range(epochs):\n",
        "        opt.zero_grad()\n",
        "        loss_fn(m(Xtr.float()), ytr.float()).backward()\n",
        "        opt.step()\n",
        "    m.eval()\n",
        "    with torch.no_grad():\n",
        "        return torch.sigmoid(m(Xte.float()))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "\"\"\"LayerMix aggregation (Paper \\u00a74, Algorithm 1; Tables 3/17).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Stage 1 SCORING: for each layer l, L2-LR probe via stratified 5-fold CV; score \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  s_l = mean AUROC. Replaces oracle selection using training data only. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Stage 2 SELECTION: L* = top-K layers (default K=5, stable for K in {3,5,7}, \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  Table 17b diffs <=0.002). Selected layers form a contiguous band (e.g. 16-20 \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  for Qwen2.5-7B). CV selection is the ONLY strategy matching the oracle; \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  Cohen's-d ranking picks scattered layers and fails (Table 17a). \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Stage 3 AGGREGATION: train f_l = LR(H(l), y; C=0.001) per selected layer on the \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  FULL train set; predict p(x) = (1/K) sum_l f_l(h(l)(x)) (Eq.1). Uniform averaging \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  = variance reduction without learned weights; dim does not scale with K. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  Cost: one forward pass + ~30s scoring (N=5k) + ~5s probe training. \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Results: 0.954 mean (matches oracle 0.952, beats middle-layer heuristic 0.941 by \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  0.013 = 15-20% fewer false positives at recall 0.90; beats all-layer avg 0.944 \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  and CLAP 0.928 in 9/9 conditions). Each rule below lives in its own \"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"  single-Impl cell; diagnostics after them are common code.\"\"\"\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT aggregation rule: layermix_predict | Paper Alg.1, 0.954 mean, matches oracle | site: per-layer states + labels -> averaged scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"LayerMix aggregation rule (Paper Alg.1: CV top-K + per-layer L2-LR + average).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Mean 0.954, matches oracle without oracle access, cost about 35 seconds.\"\"\"\n",
        "def layermix_predict(layer_states, y, K=5, C=0.001, seed=0):\n",
        "    scores = {}\n",
        "    for li, X in layer_states.items():\n",
        "        m, _ = cv_auroc_for_probe(X, y, lambda a, b, c: l2lr_fit_predict(a, b, c, C=C), k=5, seed=seed)\n",
        "        scores[li] = m\n",
        "    top = sorted(scores, key=scores.get, reverse=True)[:K]\n",
        "    probs = []\n",
        "    for li in top:\n",
        "        X = layer_states[li]\n",
        "        mu, sd = X.mean(0, keepdim=True), X.std(0, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-12)\n",
        "        Xn = (X - mu) / sd\n",
        "        w = torch.zeros(Xn.shape[1], dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "        b = torch.zeros((), dtype=torch.double)\n",
        "        lam = 1.0 / (C * len(Xn))\n",
        "        Xd, yd = Xn.double(), y.double()\n",
        "        for _ in range(2000):\n",
        "            p = torch.sigmoid(Xd @ w + b)\n",
        "            g = p - yd\n",
        "            w -= 0.5 * (Xd.t() @ g / len(Xd) + lam * w)\n",
        "            b -= 0.5 * g.mean()\n",
        "        probs.append(torch.sigmoid(Xn.double() @ w + b))\n",
        "    return sum(probs) / len(probs), top, scores\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT aggregation rule: singleoracle_predict | Paper reference 0.952, needs oracle access | site: per-layer states + labels -> best-layer scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"Single-oracle aggregation rule (Paper reference: best CV layer only, 0.952).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Needs held-out oracle access; LayerMix matches it without that cost.\"\"\"\n",
        "def singleoracle_predict(layer_states, y, C=0.001, seed=0):\n",
        "    best, best_auc = None, -1.0\n",
        "    for li, X in layer_states.items():\n",
        "        m, _ = cv_auroc_for_probe(X, y, lambda a, b, c: l2lr_fit_predict(a, b, c, C=C), k=5, seed=seed)\n",
        "        if m > best_auc:\n",
        "            best, best_auc = li, m\n",
        "    X = layer_states[best]\n",
        "    mu, sd = X.mean(0, keepdim=True), X.std(0, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-12)\n",
        "    Xn = (X - mu) / sd\n",
        "    return l2lr_fit_predict(Xn, y, Xn, C=C), [best], {best: best_auc}\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# ALWAYS-PRESENT aggregation rule: alllayeravg_predict | Paper 0.944 naive ensembling | site: per-layer states + labels -> averaged scores | plain codeblock, no Impl tag by design.\n",
        "\"\"\"All-layer-average rule (Paper: 0.944, naive ensembling without selection).\"\"\"\n",
        "\"\"\"Underperforms LayerMix: informed selection adds value beyond averaging.\"\"\"\n",
        "def alllayeravg_predict(layer_states, y, C=0.001):\n",
        "    probs = []\n",
        "    for li, X in layer_states.items():\n",
        "        mu, sd = X.mean(0, keepdim=True), X.std(0, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-12)\n",
        "        Xn = (X - mu) / sd\n",
        "        probs.append(l2lr_fit_predict(Xn, y, Xn, C=C))\n",
        "    return sum(probs) / len(probs), sorted(layer_states), {}\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
